fetchItem, url: https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/notice mergedData: {
  link: 'https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/notice',
  name: 'notice',
  description: 'notice is an English vocabulary term used as a verb for perceiving something or as a noun referring to official announcements, warnings, or time periods.',
  introduction: '## Overview\n' +
    '\n' +
    '**notice** is a fundamental English word functioning both as a verb and a noun. It encompasses everyday perception as well as formal communications in professional, legal, and personal contexts.\n' +
    '\n' +
    '### Key Meanings & Grammatical Patterns\n' +
    '\n' +
    '- **Verb Usage:** To see, hear, or become aware of something or someone (e.g., *"I noticed a subtle difference in the output"*).\n' +
    "- **Noun Usage:** Attention or awareness (*take notice*), formal notifications (*give two weeks' notice*), or posted public signs.\n" +
    '- **Common Phrases:** *notice period*, *at short notice*, *until further notice*, and *take no notice of*.\n' +
    '\n' +
    '### Practical Applications\n' +
    '\n' +
    '- **Professional & Business Writing:** Drafting resignation letters, contract termination clauses, and official administrative advisories.\n' +
    '- **Language & Communication:** Enhancing English vocabulary for academic research, writing precision, and contextual understanding.',
  categories: [ 'Writing Tools', 'Search and Research' ],
  tags: [ 'Writing', 'Productivity' ],
  image: 'https://dictionary.cambridge.org/external/images/og-image.png',
  icon: 'https://icons.duckduckgo.com/ip3/dictionary.cambridge.org.ico'
}
