fetchItem, url: https://www.investopedia.com/terms/t/three-sigma-limits.asp mergedData: {
  link: 'https://www.investopedia.com/terms/t/three-sigma-limits.asp',
  name: 'three sigma',
  description: 'Three Sigma is a statistical quality standard representing process capability where 99.73% of outputs fall within three standard deviations of the mean.',
  introduction: '# Three Sigma\n' +
    '\n' +
    'Three Sigma (3-Sigma) is a fundamental concept in statistics, manufacturing, and process engineering. It refers to the statistical rule where 99.73% of data in a normal distribution falls within three standard deviations (3σ) of the population mean.\n' +
    '\n' +
    '## Key Concepts\n' +
    '\n' +
    '- **Empirical Rule**: States that 68.27%, 95.45%, and 99.73% of values lie within one, two, and three standard deviations from the mean, respectively.\n' +
    '- **Defects Per Million**: A 3-Sigma process level corresponds to approximately 2,700 defects per million opportunities (DPMO).\n' +
    '- **Three Sigma vs. Six Sigma**: Six Sigma pushes process capability further to achieve near-perfection (3.4 DPMO), whereas Three Sigma represents standard quality control baselines.\n' +
    '\n' +
    '## Use Cases\n' +
    '\n' +
    '- **Manufacturing & Quality Assurance**: Setting product tolerance limits and identifying process anomalies.\n' +
    '- **Risk & Finance**: Detecting rare market movements, often called 3-sigma events.\n' +
    '- **Operational Efficiency**: Monitoring process performance using statistical process control charts.',
  categories: [ 'Data Tools', 'Tech Tutorials' ],
  tags: [ 'Tech', 'Tutorial', 'Productivity' ],
  image: undefined,
  icon: 'https://icons.duckduckgo.com/ip3/www.investopedia.com.ico'
}
